body:
It might be worth just moving `reloading = true;` to _after_ `ERR_FAIL_COND_V` since I don't see another instance of it in this function, but it might be safer long-term to have some kind of RAII object which resets `reloading = false` when it's destroyed as the function exits (regardless of where it exits from).

Unfortunately, it seems C++ doesn't yet have a standard "invoke this callback on scope exit" feature (though there [is a proposal](https://www.cppreference.com/w/cpp/experimental/scope_exit.html)). Two potential solutions for now could be:

1. Create an ad-hoc struct within the function:
```cpp
Error GDScript::reload(bool p_keep_state) {
  struct ReloadingGuard {
    ReloadingGuard(bool& b) : reloading{ b } { reloading = true; } // Called immediately
    ~ReloadingGuard() { reloading = false; } // Called on scope exit

    bool& reloading;
  } reloading_guard{ reloading };
  // ...
}
```

2. Create a generic implementation of the proposed "std::scope_exit" (maybe one already exists in the code base):
```cpp
class scope_exit {
public:
  template <typename Fn>
  scope_exit(Fn&& fn) noexcept
    : callback{ std::forward<Fn>(fn) }
  {}

  ~scope_exit() {
    std::invoke(callback);
  }

private:
  std::function<void(void)> callback;
};

Error GDScript::reload(bool p_keep_state) {
  reloading = true;
  scope_exit reset_reloading{
    [this]{ reloading = false; } // Called on scope exit
  };
}
```
